window.EM_DATA.lessonEnhancements = {"m1/what-is-econometrics":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Econometrics (Kinh tế lượng); Empirical analysis (Phân tích thực nghiệm); Causal effect (Tác động nhân quả); Statistical inference (Suy luận thống kê); Forecast (Dự báo). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study What Is Econometrics? is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Economic theory often predicts a direction but not a numerical magnitude. Econometrics turns a qualitative claim such as “education raises wages” into an estimable relationship and an uncertainty statement.","An empirical relationship is not automatically causal. The data-generating process, omitted factors and research design determine whether a coefficient can be interpreted as an effect."],"bullets":["Econometrics combines economic reasoning, mathematical models and statistical inference to quantify relationships with data.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Estimation, hypothesis testing and prediction are related but distinct goals. A model that predicts well can still be poor for causal interpretation, and a causal study may not maximize predictive accuracy.","The course therefore treats regression as a disciplined workflow: specify a question, build a model, collect suitable data, estimate, diagnose, infer and interpret."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Econometrics combines economic reasoning, mathematical models and statistical inference to quantify relationships with data.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Do not define econometrics as “just regression.” Explain the roles of economic theory, data and inference.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"What Is Econometrics?: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for What Is Econometrics?, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/empirical-workflow":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Hypothesis (Giả thuyết); Specification (Đặc tả mô hình); Estimation (Ước lượng); Diagnostic testing (Kiểm định chẩn đoán); Policy analysis (Phân tích chính sách). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study The Empirical Econometric Workflow is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The lecture workflow begins with a theory or hypothesis, then specifies a mathematical model and an econometric model with a stochastic error term.","Data collection comes before estimation. Variable definitions, units, sampling, time period and source quality constrain what the model can legitimately answer."],"bullets":["A defensible econometric answer is a chain of decisions, not a single regression command.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["After estimation, statistical inference evaluates uncertainty. Diagnostic checking asks whether assumptions and specification are credible enough for the intended use.","Prediction and policy analysis are downstream uses. They should not be attempted without interpreting coefficients in the model’s units and checking whether extrapolation is sensible."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A defensible econometric answer is a chain of decisions, not a single regression command.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know the order of the eight-step methodology and explain why data/model diagnostics cannot be skipped.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"The Empirical Econometric Workflow: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for The Empirical Econometric Workflow, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/economic-vs-econometric-model":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Economic model (Mô hình kinh tế); Econometric model (Mô hình kinh tế lượng); Stochastic disturbance (Sai số ngẫu nhiên); Parameter (Tham số); Estimate (Giá trị ước lượng). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Economic Model vs Econometric Model is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["An economic model summarizes a theoretical relationship, for example consumption as a function of income. It is usually an abstraction rather than an exact identity.","The econometric model introduces a stochastic disturbance u because observed outcomes vary even for units with the same included explanatory variables."],"bullets":["The econometric model adds an error term to represent influences not explicitly modeled.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The coefficients are unknown population parameters. Data are used to estimate them; the estimated equation is therefore a sample-based approximation, not the population truth itself.","This distinction explains why uncertainty is fundamental: different random samples produce different coefficient estimates."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The econometric model adds an error term to represent influences not explicitly modeled.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Be able to explain why u is required and why an estimated equation is not the true population equation.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Economic Model vs Econometric Model: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Economic Model vs Econometric Model, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/variables-error-term":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Dependent variable (Biến phụ thuộc); Regressor (Biến giải thích / biến hồi quy); Intercept (Hệ số chặn); Slope coefficient (Hệ số góc); Omitted variable (Biến bị bỏ sót). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Variables, Parameters and the Error Term is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Y is the dependent, response or explained variable. X variables are explanatory variables, regressors or predictors depending on context.","The intercept describes the conditional mean when all included X variables equal zero; whether that scenario is economically meaningful depends on the data range."],"bullets":["The error term collects unobserved determinants of Y; a regressor is useful only if its coefficient has a meaningful ceteris paribus interpretation.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The disturbance term can contain omitted determinants, measurement error, unpredictable shocks and approximation error. It should not be interpreted as “a mistake by the researcher.”","A central identification issue is whether included regressors are correlated with omitted determinants inside u. If so, ordinary least squares can be biased."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The error term collects unobserved determinants of Y; a regressor is useful only if its coefficient has a meaningful ceteris paribus interpretation.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Distinguish the population disturbance u from the sample residual û.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Variables, Parameters and the Error Term: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Variables, Parameters and the Error Term, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/data-structures":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Cross-sectional data (Dữ liệu chéo); Time-series data (Dữ liệu chuỗi thời gian); Panel data (Dữ liệu bảng); Observation (Quan sát); Frequency (Tần suất dữ liệu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Cross-Section, Time Series and Panel Data is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Cross-sectional data observe many units at roughly one point in time. Random sampling is often a useful benchmark, and the ordering of observations usually has no substantive meaning.","Time-series data observe variables over time. Chronological order carries information because current outcomes may depend on past outcomes and shocks."],"bullets":["Data structure determines which dependence patterns and econometric problems must be considered.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Panel data follow multiple cross-sectional units through time, combining both dimensions. They can reveal within-unit changes that a single cross-section cannot.","Frequency matters for time series: daily, monthly, quarterly and annual data can exhibit different seasonality, persistence and aggregation issues."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Data structure determines which dependence patterns and econometric problems must be considered.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Given a dataset description, classify the structure and state one econometric implication.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Cross-Section, Time Series and Panel Data: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Cross-Section, Time Series and Panel Data, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/measurement-data-quality":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Ratio scale (Thang đo tỷ lệ); Interval scale (Thang đo khoảng); Ordinal scale (Thang đo thứ bậc); Nominal scale (Thang đo định danh); Measurement error (Sai số đo lường). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Measurement Scales and Data Quality is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Ratio-scale variables have a meaningful zero and support ratio comparisons; interval, ordinal and nominal variables require more care in interpretation and coding.","Nominal characteristics such as category membership are often represented by dummy variables rather than treated as continuous numbers."],"bullets":["Regression output cannot repair a poorly measured or badly sampled variable.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Measurement error can enter either Y or X. Measurement error in regressors is especially important because it can alter coefficient estimates, not merely add noise.","Always document data source, unit, time period, transformations and missing-data handling before interpreting a regression."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Regression output cannot repair a poorly measured or badly sampled variable.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Do not infer quality from sample size alone; discuss measurement and representativeness.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Measurement Scales and Data Quality: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Measurement Scales and Data Quality, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/descriptive-statistics":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Sample mean (Trung bình mẫu); Variance (Phương sai); Standard deviation (Độ lệch chuẩn); Covariance (Hiệp phương sai); Correlation (Tương quan). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Descriptive Statistics, Covariance and Correlation is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Sample mean","Sample variance","Sample covariance","Sample correlation"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The sample mean summarizes location; variance and standard deviation summarize dispersion around the mean.","Covariance indicates whether two variables tend to move together, but its magnitude depends on units. Correlation rescales covariance into a unit-free number between −1 and 1."],"bullets":["Correlation standardizes covariance and measures linear association, not causality.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A correlation near zero means little linear association; it does not rule out a strong nonlinear relationship.","Before regression, descriptive statistics and scatterplots help reveal scale errors, outliers, skewness and implausible observations."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Correlation standardizes covariance and measures linear association, not causality.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know why correlation is not causation and why covariance is unit-dependent.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Descriptive Statistics, Covariance and Correlation: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Descriptive Statistics, Covariance and Correlation, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m1/probability-sampling":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Population (Tổng thể); Random sample (Mẫu ngẫu nhiên); Estimator (Ước lượng (quy tắc)); Estimate (Giá trị ước lượng); Sampling distribution (Phân phối lấy mẫu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Probability, Sampling and Sampling Distributions is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A population parameter is fixed but unknown. A statistic computed from a random sample varies from sample to sample.","An estimator is a rule for producing an estimate. The estimate is the particular numerical value obtained in one dataset."],"bullets":["An estimator is a random variable across repeated samples; its sampling distribution describes estimation uncertainty.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Expected value, variance and standard error describe the center and spread of an estimator’s sampling distribution.","Inference uses that sampling behavior to construct confidence intervals and hypothesis tests about population parameters."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: An estimator is a random variable across repeated samples; its sampling distribution describes estimation uncertainty.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Be precise: estimator is a rule/random variable; estimate is a realized number.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Probability, Sampling and Sampling Distributions: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Probability, Sampling and Sampling Distributions, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Estimator vs estimate","body":"An estimator is a rule/random variable across repeated samples; an estimate is the realized numerical value from one sample."}]},"m1/causality-correlation":{"prioritySource":"Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.; Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Ceteris paribus (Các yếu tố khác không đổi); Confounder (Yếu tố gây nhiễu); Exogeneity (Tính ngoại sinh); Endogeneity (Tính nội sinh); Reverse causality (Nhân quả ngược). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Correlation, Regression and Causality is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Regression quantifies conditional association. Causality asks what would happen to Y if X changed while relevant alternative causes were held fixed.","Omitted variables create confounding when they affect Y and are correlated with X. Reverse causality and selection can create similar problems."],"bullets":["A regression coefficient becomes a ceteris paribus causal effect only under credible exogeneity and specification conditions.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Multiple regression can control for observed confounders, but it cannot automatically control for unobserved ones.","A careful answer separates the numerical regression result from the substantive claim the research design can justify."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A regression coefficient becomes a ceteris paribus causal effect only under credible exogeneity and specification conditions.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Use “associated with” unless the assumptions/design justify “causes.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Correlation, Regression and Causality: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Correlation, Regression and Causality, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/regression-prf":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Population Regression Function (Hàm hồi quy tổng thể); Conditional mean (Trung bình có điều kiện); Slope (Hệ số góc); Intercept (Hệ số chặn); Population parameter (Tham số tổng thể). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Population Regression Function (PRF) is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Simple regression PRF"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["For the simple linear model, E(Y|X)=β₁+β₂X. The conditional mean is the average outcome among population units sharing the same X value.","β₂ is the change in the conditional mean of Y for a one-unit increase in X. Units matter: the same numerical slope can imply very different economics under different measurement scales."],"bullets":["The PRF describes how the conditional mean of Y changes with X, not where every individual observation must lie.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["β₁ is the conditional mean when X=0. It can be mathematically necessary even when X=0 lies outside the observed range and has little substantive interpretation.","Observed Y values scatter around the PRF because individual outcomes also contain the disturbance u."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The PRF describes how the conditional mean of Y changes with X, not where every individual observation must lie.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Interpret β₂ in units and explicitly refer to the conditional mean.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Population Regression Function (PRF): economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Population Regression Function (PRF), and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/stochastic-prf":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Stochastic specification (Đặc tả ngẫu nhiên); Disturbance term (Sai số ngẫu nhiên); Zero conditional mean (Kỳ vọng có điều kiện bằng 0); Systematic component (Thành phần hệ thống); Nonsystematic component (Thành phần phi hệ thống). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Stochastic Specification and the Disturbance Term is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Simple regression PRF"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Writing Y=β₁+β₂X+u turns a mean relationship into a stochastic model for individual observations.","The error term represents omitted influences, unavailable data, peripheral variables and random shocks. No practical model includes every determinant of Y."],"bullets":["The disturbance u is the gap between an individual outcome and the population conditional mean.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The zero conditional mean assumption E(u|X)=0 says that omitted influences average to zero at each X and, critically, are not systematically related to X.","If E(u|X)≠0, the slope generally mixes the effect of X with systematic omitted influences."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The disturbance u is the gap between an individual outcome and the population conditional mean.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain why E(u|X)=0 is stronger and more useful than merely saying E(u)=0.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Stochastic Specification and the Disturbance Term: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Stochastic Specification and the Disturbance Term, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/linear-in-parameters":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Linear in parameters (Tuyến tính theo tham số); Transformation (Biến đổi); Quadratic term (Hạng bậc hai); Logarithm (Logarit); Functional form (Dạng hàm). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study What “Linear” Means in Linear Regression is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Y=β₁+β₂X²+u is linear in parameters because β₁ and β₂ enter to the first power and are not multiplied together.","ln(Y)=β₁+β₂ln(X)+u is also linear in parameters. It is estimable by linear-regression methods after transforming the variables."],"bullets":["A linear regression model must be linear in the unknown coefficients; regressors themselves can be transformed.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["By contrast, a model where a parameter appears inside an exponent or denominator may be nonlinear in parameters.","This distinction opens the door to quadratic, logarithmic and interaction specifications later in the course."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A linear regression model must be linear in the unknown coefficients; regressors themselves can be transformed.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Classify models by how parameters enter, not by whether the plotted curve is straight.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"What “Linear” Means in Linear Regression: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for What “Linear” Means in Linear Regression, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/srf-residuals":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Sample Regression Function (Hàm hồi quy mẫu); Fitted value (Giá trị dự báo / giá trị khớp); Residual (Phần dư); Population error (Sai số tổng thể); Sampling fluctuation (Biến động do lấy mẫu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Sample Regression Function (SRF) and Residuals is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Residual"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The sample regression line is Ŷᵢ=β̂₁+β̂₂Xᵢ. Its coefficients are estimates obtained from the observed sample.","The residual ûᵢ=Yᵢ−Ŷᵢ measures the vertical distance between observation i and the fitted sample line."],"bullets":["The SRF estimates the PRF; a residual is observed Y minus fitted Y.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The population error uᵢ is defined relative to the unknown PRF and is not observed. The residual ûᵢ is computable and serves as an estimate of uᵢ.","Different random samples generally produce different SRFs even when they come from the same population."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The SRF estimates the PRF; a residual is observed Y minus fitted Y.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Never use u and û interchangeably; define the line to which each distance is measured.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Sample Regression Function (SRF) and Residuals: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Sample Regression Function (SRF) and Residuals, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/ols-principle":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Ordinary Least Squares (Bình phương tối thiểu thông thường); Residual Sum of Squares (Tổng bình phương phần dư); Normal equations (Hệ phương trình chuẩn); Objective function (Hàm mục tiêu); Least-squares criterion (Tiêu chuẩn bình phương tối thiểu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Ordinary Least Squares (OLS): Core Principle is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Residual Sum of Squares"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Summing residuals without squaring would allow positive and negative errors to cancel. Squaring prevents cancellation and penalizes large misses more heavily.","The minimization is over the coefficient values, not over the observed Y or X values. The data are treated as given for the fitted sample."],"bullets":["OLS chooses coefficients that minimize RSS=Σûᵢ².","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["With an intercept, the OLS normal equations imply useful identities such as Σûᵢ=0 and the fitted line passing through (X̄,Ȳ).","OLS is an estimation method. Its attractive statistical properties require additional assumptions; minimization alone does not prove unbiasedness or causality."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: OLS chooses coefficients that minimize RSS=Σûᵢ².","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State exactly what OLS minimizes and why squared residuals are used.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Ordinary Least Squares (OLS): Core Principle: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Ordinary Least Squares (OLS): Core Principle, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/derive-ols":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: First-order condition (Điều kiện bậc nhất); OLS slope estimator (Ước lượng OLS hệ số góc); OLS intercept estimator (Ước lượng OLS hệ số chặn); Sum of squares (Tổng bình phương); Identification (Khả năng nhận dạng). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Deriving the Simple OLS Estimators is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Simple OLS slope","Simple OLS intercept"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The first-order conditions set the derivatives of RSS with respect to β̂₁ and β̂₂ equal to zero.","Solving them gives β̂₂=Σ(Xᵢ−X̄)(Yᵢ−Ȳ)/Σ(Xᵢ−X̄)² and β̂₁=Ȳ−β̂₂X̄."],"bullets":["The OLS slope equals sample co-movement of X and Y divided by sample variation in X.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The slope is large when X and Y co-move strongly relative to the amount of variation in X. If X has no variation, the slope cannot be identified.","The intercept formula ensures that the fitted sample line passes through the sample means."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The OLS slope equals sample co-movement of X and Y divided by sample variation in X.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Be able to compute β̂₂ and β̂₁ from raw sums or centered data.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Deriving the Simple OLS Estimators: economic interpretation","scenario":"Using the lecture example of working time and income, compute Sxx and Sxy, obtain β̂₂=Sxy/Sxx, then β̂₁=Ȳ−β̂₂X̄ and write the SRF.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/gauss-markov":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Unbiasedness (Tính không chệch); Efficiency (Tính hiệu quả); BLUE (Ước lượng tuyến tính không chệch tốt nhất); Gauss–Markov theorem (Định lý Gauss–Markov); Sampling variance (Phương sai lấy mẫu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Unbiasedness, Efficiency and the Gauss–Markov Idea is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Unbiasedness means E(β̂ⱼ)=βⱼ over repeated samples. A single estimate can still be far from the true parameter.","Efficiency compares estimator variances. “Best” in the Gauss–Markov theorem means smallest variance among linear unbiased estimators under the stated assumptions."],"bullets":["Under the classical assumptions, OLS is Best Linear Unbiased: unbiased and minimum variance within the class of linear unbiased estimators.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Normality of the disturbance is not needed for the Gauss–Markov BLUE result itself; it is used for exact small-sample t and F inference in the classical normal model.","When homoskedasticity fails, OLS can remain unbiased under exogeneity but the usual OLS variance formula is wrong and OLS is no longer BLUE."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Under the classical assumptions, OLS is Best Linear Unbiased: unbiased and minimum variance within the class of linear unbiased estimators.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Distinguish assumptions for unbiasedness from assumptions for efficiency and exact inference.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Unbiasedness, Efficiency and the Gauss–Markov Idea: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Unbiasedness, Efficiency and the Gauss–Markov Idea, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/classical-assumptions-simple":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Homoskedasticity (Phương sai đồng nhất); Heteroskedasticity (Phương sai thay đổi); No autocorrelation (Không tự tương quan); Exogeneity (Ngoại sinh); Error variance (Phương sai sai số). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Classical Assumptions for Simple Regression is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Error variance estimator"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Linearity in parameters specifies the conditional-mean model. Variation in X is necessary to estimate a slope.","Zero conditional mean E(u|X)=0 is the key exogeneity condition for unbiased OLS under random sampling."],"bullets":["The assumptions describe the data-generating process needed for OLS interpretation and inference.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Homoskedasticity means Var(u|X)=σ² is constant across X. Heteroskedasticity allows that variance to change with X.","For time-ordered data, correlation between errors across observations violates the no-autocorrelation condition and affects standard errors."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The assumptions describe the data-generating process needed for OLS interpretation and inference.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Given a violation, state which property or inferential formula is threatened.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Classical Assumptions for Simple Regression: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Classical Assumptions for Simple Regression, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Fixed X versus random sampling","body":"Classical texts often present regressors as fixed in repeated samples. Modern introductory econometrics commonly treats a random sample of (X,Y) and states exogeneity as E(u|X)=0. The core interpretation is the same: the error must not vary systematically with the regressors."}]},"m2/standard-errors":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Standard error (Sai số chuẩn); Residual degrees of freedom (Bậc tự do phần dư); Error variance estimator (Ước lượng phương sai sai số); Precision (Độ chính xác); Sampling uncertainty (Bất định lấy mẫu). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Variance and Standard Errors of OLS is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Error variance estimator","Simple slope standard error"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The estimated disturbance variance is based on the residual sum of squares divided by residual degrees of freedom.","For the simple slope, precision improves when error variance is smaller or when X has more useful dispersion around its mean."],"bullets":["A standard error estimates the standard deviation of an estimator’s sampling distribution.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A small standard error relative to the coefficient produces a large absolute t statistic; a large standard error makes it harder to distinguish the coefficient from a hypothesized value.","Standard errors quantify sampling uncertainty under the variance formula being used. If the variance assumptions are wrong, the reported standard errors can be misleading."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A standard error estimates the standard deviation of an estimator’s sampling distribution.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain how σ² and variation in X affect Var(β̂₂).","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Variance and Standard Errors of OLS: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Variance and Standard Errors of OLS, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m2/goodness-of-fit":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 2 lecture slides — Simple Linear Regression Model (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Total Sum of Squares (Tổng bình phương toàn phần); Explained Sum of Squares (Tổng bình phương giải thích); Residual Sum of Squares (Tổng bình phương phần dư); Coefficient of determination (Hệ số xác định); Goodness of fit (Độ phù hợp). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Goodness of Fit: TSS, ESS and R² is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["R-squared"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["With an intercept, total variation decomposes as TSS=ESS+RSS, where ESS is explained variation and RSS is residual variation.","R²=ESS/TSS=1−RSS/TSS lies between 0 and 1 for the standard regression with an intercept."],"bullets":["R² is the fraction of sample variation in Y explained by the fitted regression; it is not a measure of causality.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A high R² can coexist with biased coefficients, spurious relationships or poor causal identification. A low R² can still accompany a substantively important and precisely estimated effect.","R² should be interpreted together with coefficient signs, magnitudes, uncertainty, diagnostics and the purpose of the model."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: R² is the fraction of sample variation in Y explained by the fitted regression; it is not a measure of causality.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain what an R² of 0.60 means and what it does not mean.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Goodness of Fit: TSS, ESS and R²: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Goodness of Fit: TSS, ESS and R², and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/why-multiple":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Multiple regression (Hồi quy bội); Control variable (Biến kiểm soát); Partial effect (Tác động riêng phần); Ceteris paribus (Các yếu tố khác không đổi); Omitted-variable bias (Chệch do bỏ sót biến). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Why Multiple Regression? is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In simple regression, the error term contains every omitted determinant of Y. If an omitted determinant is correlated with X, the simple slope generally does not represent the desired ceteris paribus effect.","Multiple regression moves selected relevant factors from the error term into the systematic part of the model. This can reduce omitted-variable bias when those controls are appropriately measured and specified."],"bullets":["Multiple regression isolates the partial relationship between one regressor and Y while holding the other included regressors fixed.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Adding variables is not automatically beneficial. Irrelevant regressors can reduce precision, and bad controls can distort the causal question.","The value of multiple regression is therefore conceptual control, not merely a mechanical attempt to maximize R²."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Multiple regression isolates the partial relationship between one regressor and Y while holding the other included regressors fixed.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain why the simple and multiple slopes can differ and identify the role of correlation among regressors.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Why Multiple Regression?: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Why Multiple Regression?, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/multiple-prf-srf":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Multiple PRF (Hàm hồi quy tổng thể bội); Multiple SRF (Hàm hồi quy mẫu bội); Partial slope (Hệ số góc riêng phần); Fitted value (Giá trị khớp); Residual (Phần dư). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Multiple PRF and SRF is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Multiple regression PRF","Residual"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The population model can be written Y=β₁+β₂X₂+⋯+βₖXₖ+u. The sample fitted equation replaces unknown β values by estimates β̂.","βⱼ measures the change in E(Y|X₂,…,Xₖ) from a one-unit change in Xⱼ with the remaining regressors held constant."],"bullets":["In a multiple PRF, each slope is a partial effect on the conditional mean, holding the other included regressors fixed.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The fitted value combines all included regressors at observation i. The residual remains actual Y minus fitted Y.","The distinction between population parameters and sample estimates is exactly the same as in simple regression, but each slope now depends on the full set of included regressors."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: In a multiple PRF, each slope is a partial effect on the conditional mean, holding the other included regressors fixed.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Interpret one coefficient with an explicit “holding other included variables constant” phrase.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Multiple PRF and SRF: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Multiple PRF and SRF, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/partial-coefficients":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Partial regression coefficient (Hệ số hồi quy riêng phần); Residual variation (Biến thiên phần dư); Holding constant (Giữ cố định); Conditional interpretation (Diễn giải có điều kiện); Frisch–Waugh–Lovell intuition (Trực giác Frisch–Waugh–Lovell). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Partial Regression Coefficients is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The Frisch–Waugh–Lovell intuition is to first remove from Xⱼ the component predicted by the other controls, then relate Y to the remaining residual variation.","This is why the coefficient on education in a wage equation is interpreted conditional on experience when experience is included."],"bullets":["A multiple-regression slope uses only the variation in Xⱼ that is not linearly explained by the other included regressors.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["If two regressors are highly correlated, little independent variation remains to estimate their separate partial effects, so standard errors can become large.","A partial coefficient is conditional on the chosen model. Adding or removing controls can change its meaning as well as its numerical value."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A multiple-regression slope uses only the variation in Xⱼ that is not linearly explained by the other included regressors.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State what variation identifies a partial slope and why high regressor correlation hurts precision.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Partial Regression Coefficients: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Partial Regression Coefficients, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/ols-multiple":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Coefficient vector (Vector hệ số); Design matrix (Ma trận thiết kế); Orthogonality (Tính trực giao); Invertibility (Khả nghịch); Normal equations (Hệ phương trình chuẩn). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study OLS in Multiple Regression is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Multiple OLS matrix","Residual Sum of Squares"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The residual is ûᵢ=Yᵢ−β̂₁−β̂₂X₂ᵢ−⋯−β̂ₖXₖᵢ. OLS selects the coefficient vector that minimizes Σûᵢ².","The first-order conditions make residuals orthogonal to the intercept and each included regressor in the sample."],"bullets":["Multiple OLS chooses all coefficients jointly to minimize the sum of squared residuals.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["In matrix notation the estimator is β̂=(X′X)⁻¹X′Y when X′X is invertible. Perfect multicollinearity makes that inverse unavailable.","The matrix formula is compact, but interpretation still occurs one coefficient at a time in the units and functional form of the model."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Multiple OLS chooses all coefficients jointly to minimize the sum of squared residuals.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know what perfect multicollinearity does to X′X and why the coefficients cannot be uniquely estimated.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"OLS in Multiple Regression: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for OLS in Multiple Regression, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/assumptions-multiple":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: No perfect multicollinearity (Không đa cộng tuyến hoàn hảo); Zero conditional mean (Kỳ vọng có điều kiện bằng 0); Homoskedasticity (Phương sai đồng nhất); Random sampling (Lấy mẫu ngẫu nhiên); Normality (Tính chuẩn). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Assumptions of the Multiple Regression Model is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Linearity in parameters and correct specification define the model to be estimated. Random sampling or a suitable sampling framework supports repeated-sample arguments.","No perfect collinearity requires that no regressor be an exact linear combination of the others. High but imperfect correlation is allowed, although it reduces precision."],"bullets":["The key exogeneity requirement is E(u|X₂,…,Xₖ)=0: after conditioning on all included regressors, the remaining error has zero mean.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Zero conditional mean is central for unbiasedness. Homoskedasticity and no serial correlation support the classical OLS variance formula and efficiency.","Normality is an additional distributional assumption used for exact small-sample t and F inference; large-sample methods can rely on weaker conditions."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The key exogeneity requirement is E(u|X₂,…,Xₖ)=0: after conditioning on all included regressors, the remaining error has zero mean.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Match each assumption to what it protects: identification, unbiasedness, efficiency or exact inference.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Assumptions of the Multiple Regression Model: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Assumptions of the Multiple Regression Model, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/unbiased-efficient-multiple":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Linear estimator (Ước lượng tuyến tính); Unbiased estimator (Ước lượng không chệch); Efficient estimator (Ước lượng hiệu quả); Robust standard error (Sai số chuẩn vững); Variance estimator (Ước lượng phương sai). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Unbiasedness and Efficiency in Multiple OLS is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Each β̂ⱼ is a random variable across samples. Unbiasedness is a statement about its expected value, not a guarantee that every estimate is close to βⱼ.","Estimator variance reflects noise, sample size, regressor spread and the degree to which a regressor can be predicted from the other regressors."],"bullets":["Under exogeneity OLS is unbiased; under the Gauss–Markov assumptions it is BLUE among linear unbiased estimators.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Homoskedasticity is not required for the coefficient estimates to be unbiased when exogeneity holds, but it is required for the usual homoskedastic variance formula to be correct and for the Gauss–Markov efficiency statement.","Practical empirical work should therefore report the variance estimator appropriate to the data rather than equating “OLS coefficient” with “classical standard error.”"],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Under exogeneity OLS is unbiased; under the Gauss–Markov assumptions it is BLUE among linear unbiased estimators.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["If heteroskedasticity exists but exogeneity holds, state what happens to β̂ and to conventional standard errors.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Unbiasedness and Efficiency in Multiple OLS: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Unbiasedness and Efficiency in Multiple OLS, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/variance-collinearity":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Variance inflation (Phóng đại phương sai); Collinearity (Cộng tuyến); Auxiliary regression (Hồi quy phụ); Variance Inflation Factor (Hệ số phóng đại phương sai); Precision (Độ chính xác). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Coefficient Variance and Correlation Among Regressors is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["VIF","Tolerance"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In the three-variable model, the variance formula contains a factor 1/(1−r²₂₃). As |r₂₃| approaches one, the denominator shrinks and the variance grows.","This is a precision problem rather than an automatic bias problem. High collinearity can make individual t tests weak even when the model has a high R² and a significant overall F test."],"bullets":["The variance of a partial slope increases when the regressor is highly predictable from the other regressors.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Perfect collinearity is qualitatively different: the separate coefficients are not identified at all.","The same intuition generalizes through the variance inflation factor VIFⱼ=1/(1−R²ⱼ), where R²ⱼ comes from regressing Xⱼ on the other regressors."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The variance of a partial slope increases when the regressor is highly predictable from the other regressors.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain why multicollinearity usually affects standard errors rather than unbiasedness.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Coefficient Variance and Correlation Among Regressors: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Coefficient Variance and Correlation Among Regressors, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/r2-adjusted":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Adjusted R-squared (R bình phương hiệu chỉnh); Degrees of freedom (Bậc tự do); Model fit (Độ phù hợp mô hình); Nested model (Mô hình lồng nhau); Penalty (Mức phạt). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study R² and Adjusted R² in Multiple Regression is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["R-squared","Adjusted R-squared"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Multiple R² is still 1−RSS/TSS. Because OLS can always set a new coefficient to zero, adding a regressor cannot increase the minimized RSS and therefore cannot lower R².","Adjusted R² replaces raw sums of squares by degrees-of-freedom-adjusted quantities. It can fall when a new regressor contributes too little explanatory power."],"bullets":["R² never decreases when regressors are added, so adjusted R² penalizes model size through degrees of freedom.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Neither measure proves correct specification or causality. They summarize in-sample fit under different penalties.","When comparing models, ensure they use the same dependent variable and the same observations; otherwise R² comparisons can be misleading."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: R² never decreases when regressors are added, so adjusted R² penalizes model size through degrees of freedom.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know why R² can only rise and why adjusted R² may fall after adding a regressor.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"R² and Adjusted R² in Multiple Regression: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for R² and Adjusted R² in Multiple Regression, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/functional-forms":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Functional form (Dạng hàm); Polynomial regression (Hồi quy đa thức); Marginal effect (Tác động cận biên); Interaction term (Biến tương tác); Elasticity (Độ co giãn). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Functional Forms in Multiple Regression is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Quadratic marginal effect","Quadratic turning point"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A quadratic term such as X² allows the marginal effect of X to depend on the level of X. The coefficient on X alone is not the complete marginal effect.","Log transformations often express proportional changes and elasticities more naturally than level models."],"bullets":["Multiple regression can model nonlinear economic relationships by transforming variables while remaining linear in coefficients.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Interactions allow the slope on one variable to depend on another variable or group. Main effects must then be interpreted at the reference value of the interacting variable.","Functional form should be chosen from economic reasoning, data behavior and diagnostic evidence—not by searching blindly for the highest R²."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Multiple regression can model nonlinear economic relationships by transforming variables while remaining linear in coefficients.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["For a quadratic model, compute the marginal effect and turning point rather than interpreting β₁ alone.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Functional Forms in Multiple Regression: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Functional Forms in Multiple Regression, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m3/omitted-specification":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 3 lecture slides — Multiple Regression (FTU CS2); Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Specification bias (Chệch do đặc tả); Omitted-variable bias (Chệch do bỏ sót biến); Relevant variable (Biến có liên quan); Confounding (Nhiễu); Included regressor (Biến giải thích được đưa vào). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Omitted Variables and Specification Bias is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Suppose the true model contains X₂ and X₃ but X₃ is omitted. The simple-regression coefficient on X₂ absorbs part of X₃’s effect when X₂ and X₃ are correlated.","The direction of bias depends on the sign of X₃’s effect on Y and the sign of the relationship between X₂ and X₃."],"bullets":["Omitted-variable bias requires both relevance for Y and correlation with an included regressor.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Adding a control can therefore change a coefficient substantially even if the simple regression had a high R².","Dropping a variable solely because it is statistically insignificant can create specification bias when theory says it belongs in the conditional mean."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Omitted-variable bias requires both relevance for Y and correlation with an included regressor.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State the two conditions for omitted-variable bias and reason about its likely sign.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Omitted Variables and Specification Bias: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Omitted Variables and Specification Bias, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/sampling-inference":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Point estimate (Ước lượng điểm); Standard error (Sai số chuẩn); Null hypothesis (Giả thuyết không); Alternative hypothesis (Giả thuyết đối); Significance level (Mức ý nghĩa). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study From Estimates to Statistical Inference is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A point estimate alone does not reveal precision. Standard errors connect the observed estimate to its sampling distribution.","Classical t and F procedures are derived under the regression assumptions, with normality providing exact small-sample distributions in the classical normal model."],"bullets":["Inference asks what the sample says about unknown population parameters while accounting for sampling variability.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Inference should begin with a clearly stated null and alternative hypothesis before looking at the test statistic or p value.","Statistical evidence is graded, not absolute. The significance level controls the long-run Type I error rate under the null."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Inference asks what the sample says about unknown population parameters while accounting for sampling variability.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State H0 and H1 using population coefficients, not estimated coefficients.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"From Estimates to Statistical Inference: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for From Estimates to Statistical Inference, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/individual-t":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: t statistic (Thống kê t); Critical value (Giá trị tới hạn); p value (Giá trị p); Two-sided test (Kiểm định hai phía); Fail to reject (Không bác bỏ). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study t Test for an Individual Coefficient is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["t statistic"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["For H₀:βⱼ=βⱼ,0, compute t=(β̂ⱼ−βⱼ,0)/se(β̂ⱼ). The common “zero significance” test sets βⱼ,0=0.","A large absolute t statistic is evidence against a two-sided null. The critical value depends on the significance level and residual degrees of freedom."],"bullets":["The t statistic measures how many estimated standard errors the coefficient estimate lies from its hypothesized value.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Equivalently, compare the p value with α. Reject H₀ when p<α; otherwise fail to reject H₀.","Failing to reject is not the same as proving the null true. It may reflect a small effect, a noisy estimate or insufficient sample information."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The t statistic measures how many estimated standard errors the coefficient estimate lies from its hypothesized value.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Use “fail to reject H₀,” not “accept H₀,” when evidence is insufficient.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"t Test for an Individual Coefficient: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for t Test for an Individual Coefficient, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Fail to reject ≠ accept","body":"Insufficient evidence against H₀ is reported as “fail to reject H₀.” It is not proof that the null parameter value is true."}]},"m4/one-two-tail":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: One-tailed test (Kiểm định một phía); Right-tailed test (Kiểm định phía phải); Left-tailed test (Kiểm định phía trái); Rejection region (Miền bác bỏ); Alternative hypothesis (Giả thuyết đối). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study One-Tailed and Two-Tailed Tests is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Use a two-sided test when departures in either direction matter: H₁:βⱼ≠β₀.","Use a right-tailed test when the substantive hypothesis is βⱼ>β₀ and a left-tailed test when it is βⱼ<β₀."],"bullets":["The alternative hypothesis determines whether a test is left-tailed, right-tailed or two-tailed.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A one-tailed test must be justified before seeing the estimate. Choosing the favorable tail after looking at the sign invalidates the intended error rate.","Critical values differ because the significance probability is allocated to one tail or split across two tails."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The alternative hypothesis determines whether a test is left-tailed, right-tailed or two-tailed.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Translate verbal hypotheses into inequalities before choosing a critical value.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"One-Tailed and Two-Tailed Tests: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for One-Tailed and Two-Tailed Tests, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/confidence-intervals":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Confidence interval (Khoảng tin cậy); Confidence level (Mức tin cậy); Lower bound (Cận dưới); Upper bound (Cận trên); Interval estimate (Ước lượng khoảng). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Confidence Intervals for Regression Coefficients is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Confidence interval"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A 95% confidence interval is produced by a procedure that would cover the fixed true parameter in 95% of repeated samples under the model assumptions.","For a two-sided 5% hypothesis test of H₀:βⱼ=β₀, rejecting H₀ is equivalent to β₀ lying outside the corresponding 95% confidence interval."],"bullets":["A confidence interval is β̂ⱼ ± critical value × se(β̂ⱼ).","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Interval width increases with the standard error and with more demanding confidence levels.","A confidence interval communicates both sign/magnitude uncertainty and statistical significance more richly than a p value alone."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A confidence interval is β̂ⱼ ± critical value × se(β̂ⱼ).","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Avoid saying there is a 95% probability that a fixed β lies in the realized frequentist interval.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Confidence Intervals for Regression Coefficients: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Confidence Intervals for Regression Coefficients, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Frequentist wording","body":"After a particular 95% confidence interval is computed, the fixed parameter is either inside or outside it. The 95% refers to the long-run coverage rate of the interval construction procedure."}]},"m4/overall-f":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Overall significance (Ý nghĩa tổng thể); F statistic (Thống kê F); Joint hypothesis (Giả thuyết đồng thời); Numerator degrees of freedom (Bậc tự do tử số); Denominator degrees of freedom (Bậc tự do mẫu số). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Overall Significance: the F Test is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Overall F from R-squared"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["For a model with an intercept and k−1 slopes, H₀ sets all slope coefficients equal to zero simultaneously.","The alternative says at least one slope is nonzero; it does not say every regressor is individually significant."],"bullets":["The overall F test compares explained variation with unexplained variation while accounting for model degrees of freedom.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The F statistic can be computed from R² as [R²/(k−1)]/[(1−R²)/(n−k)] under the standard notation where k includes the intercept.","A model can have a highly significant overall F test while some individual t tests are insignificant, especially under multicollinearity."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The overall F test compares explained variation with unexplained variation while accounting for model degrees of freedom.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Interpret rejection as “at least one slope differs from zero,” not “all slopes are significant.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Overall Significance: the F Test: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Overall Significance: the F Test, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/coefficient-equality":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Linear combination (Tổ hợp tuyến tính); Coefficient covariance (Hiệp phương sai hệ số); Equality restriction (Ràng buộc bằng nhau); Wald test (Kiểm định Wald); Postestimation test (Kiểm định sau ước lượng). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Testing Equality of Two Coefficients is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The numerator of the t statistic is β̂ⱼ−β̂ₗ. Its standard error depends on both variances and the covariance between the coefficient estimates.","Ignoring covariance can materially misstate uncertainty because coefficients estimated from the same regression are generally correlated."],"bullets":["Testing equality of two slopes is testing whether the difference βⱼ−βₗ equals zero.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["With one linear restriction, the equivalent F statistic equals the square of the corresponding t statistic under the same assumptions.","Software can implement the restriction directly, for example a postestimation test of coefficient equality."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Testing equality of two slopes is testing whether the difference βⱼ−βₗ equals zero.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Remember the covariance term in Var(β̂ⱼ−β̂ₗ).","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Testing Equality of Two Coefficients: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Testing Equality of Two Coefficients, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/linear-restrictions":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Restricted model (Mô hình bị ràng buộc); Unrestricted model (Mô hình không bị ràng buộc); Linear restriction (Ràng buộc tuyến tính); Constant returns to scale (Hiệu suất không đổi theo quy mô); Restricted least squares (Bình phương tối thiểu có ràng buộc). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Linear Restrictions and Restricted Least Squares is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Restricted F test"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In a Cobb–Douglas production function, constant returns to scale implies a linear restriction such as β₂+β₃=1.","One approach tests the estimated linear combination directly with a t statistic when there is a single restriction."],"bullets":["A restricted model imposes H₀ on the coefficients; an unrestricted model estimates them freely.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A general F test compares RSS from the restricted and unrestricted models. If imposing the restriction worsens fit too much relative to unrestricted residual variance, reject the restriction.","For m restrictions, F=[(RSS_R−RSS_UR)/m]/[RSS_UR/(n−k)], where k is the number of parameters in the unrestricted model."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A restricted model imposes H₀ on the coefficients; an unrestricted model estimates them freely.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Use RSS_R ≥ RSS_UR and put the unrestricted RSS in the denominator variance estimate.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Linear Restrictions and Restricted Least Squares: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Linear Restrictions and Restricted Least Squares, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/chow-test":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Structural break (Điểm gãy cấu trúc); Parameter stability (Ổn định tham số); Pooled regression (Hồi quy gộp); Subsample (Mẫu con); Chow test (Kiểm định Chow). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Chow Test for Structural Stability is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Chow F test"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Estimate the pooled restricted model and the two unrestricted subgroup regressions under the classical setup.","The null says the relevant regression coefficients are the same across groups or periods; the alternative allows structural change."],"bullets":["The Chow test asks whether pooling two subsamples under one common coefficient vector loses too much fit relative to estimating them separately.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The test is an F comparison based on the increase in RSS caused by imposing common coefficients.","Interpretation requires caution: heteroskedasticity or other assumption failures can invalidate the classical Chow test, and the break point should have substantive justification."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The Chow test asks whether pooling two subsamples under one common coefficient vector loses too much fit relative to estimating them separately.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State precisely which coefficients are assumed equal under H₀ and how many restrictions are imposed.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Chow Test for Structural Stability: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Chow Test for Structural Stability, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/functional-form-tests":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Specification test (Kiểm định đặc tả); RESET test (Kiểm định RESET); Functional form (Dạng hàm); Nonlinearity (Phi tuyến); Model adequacy (Mức phù hợp của mô hình). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Testing Functional Form is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A linear-looking economic question may require logs, squares or interactions. Residual patterns and theory can signal missing nonlinear structure.","RESET-style logic adds functions of fitted values and tests whether they contribute jointly, providing a broad specification diagnostic."],"bullets":["Functional-form tests ask whether omitted nonlinear combinations of the regressors add explanatory power.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Rejection indicates the current specification is inadequate but does not tell you the unique correct alternative model.","When comparing linear and log specifications, pay attention to the dependent variable scale; R² values are not directly comparable if the dependent variable differs."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Functional-form tests ask whether omitted nonlinear combinations of the regressors add explanatory power.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["A rejected specification test diagnoses a problem; it does not identify the cure by itself.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Testing Functional Form: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Testing Functional Form, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m4/stat-economic-significance":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Statistical significance (Ý nghĩa thống kê); Economic significance (Ý nghĩa kinh tế); Effect size (Kích thước tác động); Precision (Độ chính xác); External validity (Tính khái quát bên ngoài). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Statistical vs Economic Significance is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A tiny coefficient can be statistically significant in a large precise sample but economically negligible.","A large economically important estimate can fail to reach conventional significance when the standard error is large."],"bullets":["Statistical significance is about signal relative to sampling uncertainty; economic significance is about magnitude, units and consequences.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Interpretation should therefore report the coefficient in meaningful units and, when possible, a confidence interval—not only stars or p values.","Policy conclusions need both statistical evidence and substantive reasoning about costs, benefits, baseline levels and external validity."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Statistical significance is about signal relative to sampling uncertainty; economic significance is about magnitude, units and consequences.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Never equate p<0.05 with “important” or p>0.05 with “no effect.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Statistical vs Economic Significance: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Statistical vs Economic Significance, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/multicollinearity-nature":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Multicollinearity (Đa cộng tuyến); Perfect multicollinearity (Đa cộng tuyến hoàn hảo); Imperfect multicollinearity (Đa cộng tuyến không hoàn hảo); Linear dependence (Phụ thuộc tuyến tính); Pairwise correlation (Tương quan từng cặp). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study The Nature of Multicollinearity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["VIF","Tolerance"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Perfect multicollinearity exists when one regressor is an exact linear combination of others. Then the model cannot separately identify their coefficients.","Imperfect multicollinearity means regressors are strongly but not perfectly related. OLS can still be computed, but independent variation is scarce."],"bullets":["Multicollinearity concerns linear dependence among explanatory variables; perfect collinearity prevents unique OLS estimation, while high collinearity mainly harms precision.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Multicollinearity is a property of the regressor data, not the disturbance term. It can arise naturally when economic variables move together or when variables are mechanically constructed from one another.","High pairwise correlation is one warning sign, but multicollinearity can involve combinations of several variables even when no single pairwise correlation appears extreme."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Multicollinearity concerns linear dependence among explanatory variables; perfect collinearity prevents unique OLS estimation, while high collinearity mainly harms precision.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Do not say multicollinearity causes bias by itself; distinguish non-identification from imprecision.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"The Nature of Multicollinearity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for The Nature of Multicollinearity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/multicollinearity-consequences":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Inflated variance (Phương sai bị phóng đại); Wide confidence interval (Khoảng tin cậy rộng); Insignificant t ratio (Tỷ số t không có ý nghĩa); Sensitivity (Độ nhạy); Joint significance (Ý nghĩa đồng thời). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Consequences of Multicollinearity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["VIF"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["As collinearity rises, confidence intervals widen and absolute t statistics often shrink because standard errors become larger.","The overall R² and F statistic can still be high even when several individual t tests are insignificant. The regressors may jointly explain Y while their separate contributions are hard to disentangle."],"bullets":["High multicollinearity inflates variances and covariances of OLS coefficient estimates, making individual effects difficult to estimate precisely.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Coefficient estimates and standard errors can become sensitive to small data changes or to the inclusion and exclusion of correlated regressors.","Wrong signs or unstable magnitudes can be symptoms, but they are not definitive proof; economic theory and diagnostic evidence are still needed."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: High multicollinearity inflates variances and covariances of OLS coefficient estimates, making individual effects difficult to estimate precisely.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain the pattern “high R² + significant F + insignificant individual t tests” without claiming it proves multicollinearity.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Consequences of Multicollinearity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Consequences of Multicollinearity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/multicollinearity-detection":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Auxiliary regression (Hồi quy phụ); VIF (Hệ số phóng đại phương sai); Tolerance (Hệ số dung sai); Diagnostic threshold (Ngưỡng chẩn đoán); Rule of thumb (Quy tắc kinh nghiệm). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Detecting Multicollinearity: Correlation, Auxiliary Regressions and VIF is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["VIF","Tolerance"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Pairwise correlations above a high threshold such as 0.8 can signal a problem but are only a sufficient warning, not a complete multivariate diagnostic.","An auxiliary regression predicts one regressor from all remaining regressors. Its R²ⱼ enters the VIF and tolerance formulas."],"bullets":["VIFⱼ=1/(1−R²ⱼ) measures how much the variance of β̂ⱼ is inflated because Xⱼ is linearly related to the other regressors.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["VIF becomes large as R²ⱼ approaches one. The common VIF>10 threshold is a rule of thumb, not a universal law.","Tolerance is 1/VIF=1−R²ⱼ. Small tolerance means little independent variation remains in that regressor."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: VIFⱼ=1/(1−R²ⱼ) measures how much the variance of β̂ⱼ is inflated because Xⱼ is linearly related to the other regressors.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Given R²ⱼ, calculate VIF and tolerance and explain what they mean.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Detecting Multicollinearity: Correlation, Auxiliary Regressions and VIF: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Detecting Multicollinearity: Correlation, Auxiliary Regressions and VIF, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"VIF thresholds are heuristics","body":"VIF>10 and pairwise correlation above 0.8 are common rules of thumb in the supplied material, not universal theorem-level cutoffs. Context, sample size and research purpose matter."}]},"m5/multicollinearity-remedies":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Remedial measure (Biện pháp khắc phục); A priori information (Thông tin tiên nghiệm); Reparameterization (Tái tham số hóa); Specification bias (Chệch đặc tả); Additional data (Dữ liệu bổ sung). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Responding to Multicollinearity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Sometimes the best response is to do nothing, especially when prediction is the goal or the correlated regressors are required by theory and the coefficients of interest remain sufficiently precise.","Additional data can help if they provide new independent variation rather than simply repeating the same collinear pattern."],"bullets":["The remedy for multicollinearity should preserve the substantive model whenever possible; deleting a relevant variable can create worse specification bias.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Prior restrictions, variable transformations, combining data sources or reparameterization can reduce uncertainty in justified settings.","Dropping a variable solely to improve t statistics is dangerous: if the omitted variable belongs in the conditional mean and correlates with included regressors, omitted-variable bias can result."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The remedy for multicollinearity should preserve the substantive model whenever possible; deleting a relevant variable can create worse specification bias.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Choose the least damaging remedy and explain why “drop the insignificant variable” is not a general rule.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Responding to Multicollinearity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Responding to Multicollinearity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/heteroskedasticity-nature":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Heteroskedasticity (Phương sai thay đổi); Homoskedasticity (Phương sai đồng nhất); Conditional variance (Phương sai có điều kiện); Outlier (Ngoại lệ); Residual plot (Đồ thị phần dư). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study The Nature of Heteroskedasticity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Cross-sectional economic data often exhibit changing variance because larger households, firms or incomes have more room for absolute variation.","Outliers, omitted variables, skewed regressors and incorrect functional form can also generate heteroskedastic-looking residual patterns."],"bullets":["Heteroskedasticity means Var(uᵢ|Xᵢ) changes across observations instead of remaining equal to a common σ².","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["The mean model and the variance model are different objects: a regression can have the correct conditional mean but nonconstant error variance.","Residual plots are useful because sample residuals are observable proxies for the unobservable population errors."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Heteroskedasticity means Var(uᵢ|Xᵢ) changes across observations instead of remaining equal to a common σ².","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["State heteroskedasticity as a variance condition, not as “the residuals are large.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"The Nature of Heteroskedasticity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for The Nature of Heteroskedasticity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/heteroskedasticity-consequences":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error (Sai số chuẩn vững với phương sai thay đổi); Robust standard error (Sai số chuẩn vững); Weighted Least Squares (Bình phương tối thiểu có trọng số); Generalized Least Squares (Bình phương tối thiểu tổng quát); Efficiency loss (Mất hiệu quả). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study OLS under Heteroskedasticity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The OLS coefficient formula does not require equal variance to exist. The problem is that the classical variance formula assumes a common σ² and therefore mismeasures uncertainty when variances differ.","Consequently, conventional t and F statistics based on those standard errors can be misleading."],"bullets":["With exogeneity, OLS coefficients remain linear and unbiased under heteroskedasticity, but OLS is no longer efficient and conventional homoskedastic standard errors are unreliable.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Robust (heteroskedasticity-consistent) standard errors keep the OLS coefficient estimates but replace the variance estimator.","Weighted or generalized least squares can be more efficient when the variance structure is modeled credibly, but misspecified weights can create new problems."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: With exogeneity, OLS coefficients remain linear and unbiased under heteroskedasticity, but OLS is no longer efficient and conventional homoskedastic standard errors are unreliable.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["If heteroskedasticity exists under exogeneity, say “OLS unbiased but conventional SEs unreliable,” not “OLS is biased.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"OLS under Heteroskedasticity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for OLS under Heteroskedasticity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/heteroskedasticity-detection":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Breusch–Pagan test (Kiểm định Breusch–Pagan); White test (Kiểm định White); Park test (Kiểm định Park); Glejser test (Kiểm định Glejser); Lagrange Multiplier statistic (Thống kê nhân tử Lagrange). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Detecting Heteroskedasticity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["LM nR-squared"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Graphically, plot residuals or squared residuals against fitted values or regressors and look for systematic changes in spread such as funnels or curved variance patterns.","Park and Glejser procedures impose particular auxiliary relationships. The Breusch–Pagan approach uses an auxiliary regression of squared residuals and an LM statistic related to nR²."],"bullets":["Formal heteroskedasticity tests regress functions of residuals on variables suspected to explain the error variance.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["White’s test is more general because it can include squares and cross-products, but a large auxiliary model can consume degrees of freedom.","A significant test is evidence against homoskedasticity, but it does not automatically identify whether the underlying cause is scale, outliers or misspecification."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Formal heteroskedasticity tests regress functions of residuals on variables suspected to explain the error variance.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know the null hypothesis of homoskedasticity and compute LM=nR² when that version of the test applies.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Detecting Heteroskedasticity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Detecting Heteroskedasticity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/heteroskedasticity-remedies":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Feasible GLS (GLS khả thi); Weight (Trọng số); Variance function (Hàm phương sai); Robust inference (Suy luận vững); Transformation (Biến đổi). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Correcting for Heteroskedasticity is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["If the form of Var(uᵢ|Xᵢ) is known up to scale, transforming the model by the standard deviation of the error yields a weighted least-squares problem.","When the variance function is unknown, feasible GLS estimates it first and then constructs weights. Its efficiency advantage depends on that variance model being credible."],"bullets":["When the mean specification is credible, robust standard errors are often the direct fix for inference; WLS/GLS additionally changes coefficient weighting.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors require less structure: keep β̂_OLS and adjust only the estimated covariance matrix.","Also reconsider functional form and data quality. A variance diagnostic may be a symptom of an omitted transformation or an influential observation rather than a standalone variance problem."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: When the mean specification is credible, robust standard errors are often the direct fix for inference; WLS/GLS additionally changes coefficient weighting.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Distinguish “change the standard errors” from “change the estimator/weights.”","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Correcting for Heteroskedasticity: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Correcting for Heteroskedasticity, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/autocorrelation-nature":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Autocorrelation (Tự tương quan); Serial correlation (Tương quan chuỗi); AR(1) error (Sai số AR(1)); Positive autocorrelation (Tự tương quan dương); Innovation (Nhiễu mới / innovation). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study The Nature of Autocorrelation is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Durbin-Watson","Approximate rho from DW"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Positive first-order autocorrelation means a positive disturbance tends to be followed by another positive disturbance; negative autocorrelation tends to alternate signs.","Common causes include inertia, omitted time patterns, incorrect functional form, data smoothing and aggregation."],"bullets":["Autocorrelation exists when Cov(uₜ,uₛ)≠0 for different time observations, often because shocks persist over time.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Autocorrelation is especially relevant for time-series data because chronological dependence is part of the data-generating process.","A common AR(1) representation is uₜ=ρuₜ₋₁+εₜ with |ρ|<1 for a stationary error process."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Autocorrelation exists when Cov(uₜ,uₛ)≠0 for different time observations, often because shocks persist over time.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Distinguish autocorrelation in errors from a lagged relationship in the observed Y series.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"The Nature of Autocorrelation: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for The Nature of Autocorrelation, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m5/autocorrelation-detection-remedies":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 5 lecture slides — Diagnosing Model Problems (FTU CS2); Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Durbin–Watson statistic (Thống kê Durbin–Watson); Breusch–Godfrey test (Kiểm định Breusch–Godfrey); Inconclusive region (Vùng không kết luận); Newey–West standard error (Sai số chuẩn Newey–West); Dynamic specification (Đặc tả động). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Detecting and Correcting Autocorrelation is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Durbin-Watson","Approximate rho from DW"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Plot residuals in time order first. Long runs of the same sign or smooth waves suggest positive serial correlation; alternating signs suggest negative serial correlation.","The Durbin–Watson statistic is approximately 2(1−ρ̂) for first-order correlation: values near 2 suggest little first-order autocorrelation, below 2 suggest positive, and above 2 suggest negative correlation."],"bullets":["Serial-correlation diagnostics should match the model: Durbin–Watson is specialized, while Breusch–Godfrey handles broader lag structures.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Durbin–Watson has assumptions and tabulated inconclusive regions and is not a universal test, especially when a lagged dependent variable is present. Breusch–Godfrey is more flexible for higher-order correlation.","Potential remedies include improving the dynamic specification, using GLS-type transformations when a serial-correlation model is credible, or using autocorrelation-consistent standard errors such as Newey–West for inference in appropriate settings."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Serial-correlation diagnostics should match the model: Durbin–Watson is specialized, while Breusch–Godfrey handles broader lag structures.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Do not apply a simplistic universal DW cutoff; know what d≈2, d<2 and d>2 suggest and when BG is preferable.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Detecting and Correcting Autocorrelation: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Detecting and Correcting Autocorrelation, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Durbin–Watson has conditions","body":"d≈2 is useful intuition, but the formal Durbin–Watson decision uses model-specific bounds and can be inconclusive. It is not appropriate as a universal test, particularly with a lagged dependent variable; Breusch–Godfrey is more flexible."}]},"m6/log-models":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Log-level model (Mô hình log-mức); Level-log model (Mô hình mức-log); Log-log model (Mô hình log-log); Semielasticity (Bán co giãn); Elasticity (Độ co giãn). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Logarithmic Functional Forms is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Exact semilog percentage"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In a level-level model Y=β₀+β₁X+u, β₁ is the change in Y units for a one-unit change in X.","In log(Y)=β₀+β₁X+u, a one-unit rise in X changes Y by approximately 100β₁ percent for small β₁; exact percentage effects use 100[exp(β₁ΔX)−1]."],"bullets":["The meaning of a coefficient depends on which variables are logged; units determine whether it is a unit effect, semielasticity or elasticity.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["In Y=β₀+β₁log(X)+u, a 1% rise in X changes Y by approximately β₁/100 units. In log-log models β₁ is an elasticity: a 1% X increase changes Y by β₁%.","Interpretation must use the actual unit change in the question. A ten-unit change in X multiplies the log-level exponent by ten."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: The meaning of a coefficient depends on which variables are logged; units determine whether it is a unit effect, semielasticity or elasticity.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Identify the functional form before interpreting any coefficient; use the exact exponential effect when requested.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Logarithmic Functional Forms: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Logarithmic Functional Forms, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/dummy-variables":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Dummy variable (Biến giả); Reference group (Nhóm tham chiếu); Dummy-variable trap (Bẫy biến giả); Group effect (Tác động nhóm); Exact percentage effect (Tác động phần trăm chính xác). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Dummy Variables and Reference Groups is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Exact semilog percentage"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["A dummy variable takes values 0 and 1. With an intercept, including every category dummy creates perfect multicollinearity, so one category must be omitted as the reference.","In a level dependent-variable model, the dummy coefficient is the conditional difference in Y units between the group coded 1 and the reference group."],"bullets":["A dummy coefficient compares the indicated group with the omitted reference group, holding other regressors fixed.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["In a log(Y) model, the exact percentage group difference is 100[exp(δ)−1], not exactly 100δ unless δ is small.","Changing the reference group changes coefficient labels and signs but does not change fitted values or the underlying group comparisons."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A dummy coefficient compares the indicated group with the omitted reference group, holding other regressors fixed.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["For a dummy in log(Y), distinguish the approximation 100δ% from the exact 100(e^δ−1)%.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Dummy Variables and Reference Groups: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Dummy Variables and Reference Groups, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Exact dummy effect in log(Y)","body":"For a dummy coefficient δ in a log-dependent-variable model, the exact conditional percentage difference is 100[exp(δ)−1]%, while 100δ% is an approximation."}]},"m6/quadratic-models":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Gujarati & Porter, Basic Econometrics, 5th ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Quadratic term (Hạng bậc hai); Marginal effect (Tác động cận biên); Turning point (Điểm ngoặt); Concave (Lõm); Convex (Lồi). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Quadratic Terms and Turning Points is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":["Quadratic marginal effect","Quadratic turning point"],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The coefficient β₁ is the marginal effect only at X=0. At other values, the X² term changes the slope.","If β₂<0, the parabola is concave and the turning point is a maximum; if β₂>0, it is convex and the turning point is a minimum."],"bullets":["In Y=β₀+β₁X+β₂X²+…, the marginal effect is β₁+2β₂X and the turning point is −β₁/(2β₂).","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["A turning point is substantively useful only if it lies within a meaningful range of observed X values.","When X² is included, keep the lower-order X term unless a specific theory justifies omitting it."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: In Y=β₀+β₁X+β₂X²+…, the marginal effect is β₁+2β₂X and the turning point is −β₁/(2β₂).","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Compute the marginal effect at the X value given in the question, not β₁ alone.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Quadratic Terms and Turning Points: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Quadratic Terms and Turning Points, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/interactions":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach, 3rd ed.","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Interaction effect (Tác động tương tác); Main effect (Tác động chính); Group-specific slope (Hệ số góc theo nhóm); Marginal effect (Tác động cận biên); Linear combination (Tổ hợp tuyến tính). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Interaction Terms is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["For Y=β₀+β₁X+β₂D+β₃(XD)+u, the slope on X is β₁ for D=0 and β₁+β₃ for D=1.","The coefficient on D is the group difference when X=0. If X=0 is not meaningful, centering X can make the main effect easier to interpret."],"bullets":["With an interaction X×Z, the marginal effect of X depends on Z and the main effects are conditional on the interacting variable being zero.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["An interaction is not “just another control.” It changes the meaning of both participating main-effect coefficients.","Hypotheses about group-specific slopes often require testing linear combinations such as β₁+β₃."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: With an interaction X×Z, the marginal effect of X depends on Z and the main effects are conditional on the interacting variable being zero.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Write the fitted equation separately for D=0 and D=1 before interpreting an interaction.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Interaction Terms: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Interaction Terms, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/centering":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Centering (Định tâm biến); Reference value (Giá trị tham chiếu); Reparameterization (Tái tham số hóa); Benchmark (Mốc so sánh); Fitted value invariance (Tính bất biến của giá trị khớp). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Centering Variables for Interpretation is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In an interaction model, the dummy main effect is evaluated at X=0. If zero is outside the relevant range, that coefficient can be hard to interpret.","Define Xc=X−c so that Xc=0 corresponds to a meaningful value such as 12 years of education. Then the group dummy coefficient describes the gap at that benchmark."],"bullets":["Replacing X by X−c changes the intercept and main-effect interpretation but not fitted values or the underlying interaction slopes.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Centering does not “fix” multicollinearity in the general econometric sense; with polynomial or interaction terms it can reduce nonessential correlation and improve numerical interpretation.","Always state the new reference point explicitly after centering."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Replacing X by X−c changes the intercept and main-effect interpretation but not fitted values or the underlying interaction slopes.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Explain what the dummy coefficient means after recentering and why predictions remain unchanged.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Centering Variables for Interpretation: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Centering Variables for Interpretation, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/regression-tables":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Chapter 4 lecture slides — Statistical Inference (Le Hang My Hanh)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Regression output (Kết quả hồi quy); Coefficient table (Bảng hệ số); Root MSE (Căn MSE); Overall F test (Kiểm định F tổng thể); Confidence interval (Khoảng tin cậy). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Reading a Regression Output Table is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["Start with the dependent variable, sample size and model specification. Then read each coefficient together with its standard error, t statistic or p value and confidence interval.","R² and adjusted R² describe fit; the overall F test addresses joint significance. Neither substitutes for coefficient interpretation."],"bullets":["A regression table should be read in layers: model/sample, coefficient magnitude and sign, standard errors/test statistics, fit, and diagnostics.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Check units and transformations before translating a coefficient into words. A coefficient of 0.05 has completely different meaning in level-level and log-log models.","Finish with assumptions and limitations. A statistically significant table can still be misleading under omitted-variable bias, heteroskedasticity or serial correlation."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A regression table should be read in layers: model/sample, coefficient magnitude and sign, standard errors/test statistics, fit, and diagnostics.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Use a consistent order: equation → sign/magnitude → significance → fit → diagnostics → economic interpretation.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a Regression Output Table: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Reading a Regression Output Table, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/stata-eviews-workflow":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials; Stata official regression postestimation documentation","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Postestimation (Phân tích sau ước lượng); Regression command (Lệnh hồi quy); Residual diagnostic (Chẩn đoán phần dư); Reproducibility (Khả năng tái lập); Specification record (Ghi chép đặc tả). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Stata / EViews Regression Workflow is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["In Stata, a basic OLS workflow starts with inspecting data, estimating with regress, then using postestimation tools appropriate to the model and data structure.","Common course diagnostics include variance inflation factors for multicollinearity, heteroskedasticity tests, residual plots and time-series serial-correlation tests such as Durbin–Watson or Breusch–Godfrey when their conditions apply."],"bullets":["Software executes an econometric procedure; it does not choose the research question, specification or interpretation for you.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["In EViews, the same logic appears through equation estimation and residual/diagnostic views. The menu path differs, but the econometric null hypotheses are the same.","A reproducible workflow records commands, variable transformations, sample restrictions and the exact specification rather than relying only on screenshots."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Software executes an econometric procedure; it does not choose the research question, specification or interpretation for you.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Know what each diagnostic is testing; memorizing software clicks is not enough.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Stata / EViews Regression Workflow: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Stata / EViews Regression Workflow, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[{"title":"Software output is not an assumption check by itself","body":"Commands such as estat vif, estat hettest, estat dwatson and estat bgodfrey implement specific diagnostics. Their output is meaningful only when the test’s assumptions and null hypothesis fit the model."}]},"m6/empirical-project":{"prioritySource":"Chapter 1 lecture slides — Introduction to Econometrics (Le Hang My Hanh, FTU CS2)","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Literature review (Tổng quan tài liệu); Methodology (Phương pháp nghiên cứu); Summary statistics (Thống kê mô tả); Empirical results (Kết quả thực nghiệm); Appendix (Phụ lục). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Empirical Project and Report Structure is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["The course project structure includes abstract, introduction, literature review, methodology and data, results, conclusion, references and appendices.","The methodology section defines the dependent and independent variables, expected signs and econometric equation. The data section documents sources and summary statistics."],"bullets":["A strong report links one research question consistently through literature, model, data, estimation, diagnostics and conclusion.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["Results should report a clean estimation table and interpret both statistical and economic significance, followed by relevant diagnostic checks and any justified corrections.","The conclusion answers the original research question without claiming more causal certainty than the design supports and acknowledges limitations."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: A strong report links one research question consistently through literature, model, data, estimation, diagnostics and conclusion.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Keep every section tied to the research question; do not present output without interpretation.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Empirical Project and Report Structure: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Empirical Project and Report Structure, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]},"m6/exam-strategy":{"prioritySource":"Supplied FTU Econometrics final-exam / test-bank materials","moreExplanation":["Key vocabulary used in this lesson: Exam protocol (Quy trình làm bài thi); Model identification (Nhận dạng mô hình); Decision rule (Quy tắc quyết định); Diagnostic sequence (Chuỗi chẩn đoán); Plausibility check (Kiểm tra tính hợp lý). Keep the English term visible because textbooks, Stata/EViews output and exam questions normally use it; use the Vietnamese translation as a precision aid.","A useful way to study Econometrics Exam Strategy is to separate three layers: the population statement, what the sample estimator actually computes, and the inferential claim that is justified only under assumptions. Keeping these layers separate prevents many econometrics exam errors.","When you write an answer, translate the mathematics back into economic language. Name the dependent variable, the regressor or restriction involved, the units of change, the variables held fixed, and the uncertainty or diagnostic condition that limits the conclusion."],"termsExtra":[],"formulaKeys":[],"deepDive":[{"kicker":"Meaning","title":"What the concept is really saying","paragraphs":["First identify the model form and data type. Circle logs, squares, dummies and interactions because they change coefficient interpretation.","For hypothesis tests, write H₀ and H₁, compute or read the statistic, compare with the correct critical value/p value, then conclude in the language of the economic question."],"bullets":["Most exam errors come from misidentifying the model, null hypothesis, units or violated assumption before calculation.","Population target: identify what unknown feature of E(Y|X) or Var(u|X) this concept concerns.","Sample evidence: identify the estimator, statistic, residual pattern or regression output used to learn about it."]},{"kicker":"Mechanism","title":"Why the result works — and when it fails","paragraphs":["For diagnostics, separate nature → consequence → detection → remedy. This prevents common confusions such as saying heteroskedasticity biases OLS coefficients under exogeneity.","For numerical work, write the formula symbolically before inserting numbers and check direction, units, degrees of freedom and whether the answer is economically plausible."],"bullets":["Assumption check: ask which conditions are needed for the coefficient, standard error or test to have its stated interpretation.","Unit check: interpret coefficients using the actual scale or transformation of Y and X.","Specification check: ask whether an omitted variable, nonlinear term, interaction or dynamic component could change the conclusion."]},{"kicker":"Exam reasoning","title":"How to turn the concept into a full-credit answer","paragraphs":["Start with the governing rule: Most exam errors come from misidentifying the model, null hypothesis, units or violated assumption before calculation.","Then connect the rule to the question’s numbers or regression output. Finish with a contextual conclusion rather than stopping at a statistic or p value."],"bullets":["Use the four-step structure: identify → calculate/test → interpret → diagnose/check.","Write H₀/H₁ explicitly when inference is involved.","For diagnostics, use the sequence nature → consequence → detection → remedy.","For functional forms, identify logs, squares, dummies and interactions before interpreting coefficients."]}],"examples":[{"title":"Econometrics Exam Strategy: economic interpretation","scenario":"Use a small economic example to identify the variables, state the relevant rule for Econometrics Exam Strategy, and explain the conclusion in context.","steps":["Identify the population relationship or assumption being studied.","Write the relevant equation, statistic or diagnostic rule before inserting numbers.","Interpret the result in economic units and state any ceteris paribus condition.","Add the assumption or limitation that determines whether the conclusion is descriptive, inferential or causal."],"result":"A complete answer contains the rule, calculation/logic, contextual interpretation and limitation.","takeaway":"Econometrics marks are often lost after the arithmetic. The interpretation and assumption statement are part of the answer."}],"sourceCorrections":[]}};
